HESS — CANONICAL GAME RULES V3

Document 2 — Core Rules Specification
Status: Active / Living Canon
Relationship: Child specification of HESS_REBUILD_SPECIFICATION_V3.md
Purpose: Define the current playable rules of Hess independently of the existing codebase.


---

1. PURPOSE

This document defines the current canonical rules of Hess.

It is intended to serve as the primary rules reference for:

the game engine,

multiplayer validation,

AI simulation,

testing,

UI/UX,

tutorials,

Piece Academy,

and future rule development.


This document describes what Hess does.

It does not prescribe how the software must implement those rules.

Detailed individual piece movement is intentionally separated into the future:

> Document 3 — Pieces & Movement Specification



Special mechanics will also receive deeper treatment in a dedicated document.


---

2. RULE STATUS SYSTEM

Because Hess is still evolving, every rule may have one of four statuses.

CANONICAL

The rule is currently established and should be implemented as written.

PROVISIONAL

The rule is sufficiently understood to require deterministic implementation, but may be revised after playtesting.

OPEN

The intended behavior has not yet been completely resolved.

DISCOVERED ISSUE

The existing implementation exhibits behavior that conflicts with the intended rules or creates an unresolved edge case.

An OPEN rule must not be silently guessed by the implementation.

Instead, a Provisional Resolution should be chosen where necessary so that the rebuilt game remains playable.


---

3. CORE DESIGN PRINCIPLE

Hess uses the chessboard and familiar strategic concepts as its foundation, but Hess is not conventional chess.

Traditional chess assumptions must therefore not automatically apply.

The game engine must always ask:

> “What does Hess permit?”



rather than:

> “What would chess permit?”




---

4. THE BOARD

Hess is played on an 8 × 8 board.

The board consists of:

8 files: a–h

8 ranks: 1–8


Squares are therefore identified using standard coordinate notation:

a1 ... h1
a2 ... h2
...
a8 ... h8

The board contains a conceptual midfield separating the two halves.

The midfield lies between:

Rank 4
───────
MIDFIELD
───────
Rank 5

The midfield is a rule boundary for specific mechanics.

It does not automatically restrict every piece.


---

5. PLAYERS AND SIDES

Hess has two opposing players:

White

Black


Each player controls their own pieces.

White traditionally occupies the lower side of the board, while Black occupies the upper side.

However, Hess's setup mechanics can alter the initial arrangement.


---

6. STARTING SETUP

Hess supports a hidden setup phase.

Before the game begins, each player may arrange their permitted starting pieces according to the setup rules.

The opponent must not be able to observe the player's private setup decisions during this phase.

Important distinction

The player's private setup state must remain private on the server.

It is not sufficient for the client merely to visually hide it.

The server must not unnecessarily transmit the opponent's hidden arrangement.


---

7. SETUP REVEAL

The setup phase ends when both players have confirmed that they are ready.

Conceptually:

White chooses setup
        ↓
White Ready

Black chooses setup
        ↓
Black Ready

        ↓

Both setups revealed
        ↓
Game begins

The complete resulting starting position becomes visible when the rules permit the reveal.


---

8. TURN STRUCTURE

Hess is turn-based.

Players alternate turns unless a special mechanic explicitly causes the same player to retain the turn.

A normal turn consists of:

Player receives turn
       ↓
Player selects legal action
       ↓
Action is validated
       ↓
Action is resolved
       ↓
Special state checked
       ↓
Game-end conditions checked
       ↓
Next player receives turn

A special action may interrupt this sequence.

Therefore the engine must not assume:

> Every completed action automatically changes the active player.



This is particularly important for Jester interactions.


---

9. LEGAL ACTIONS

A player may only perform an action permitted by the current game state.

An action must satisfy:

1. The player owns the acting piece.


2. The piece is capable of performing that action.


3. The destination or target is valid.


4. The action does not violate Hess's relevant restrictions.


5. Any required special state is active.


6. Any required resource or opportunity exists.


7. The resulting position is legal according to Hess.



The server is authoritative for multiplayer games.


---

10. MOVEMENT

Each Hess piece has its own movement rules.

The complete movement definitions belong to the Piece Specification.

In general:

> A piece may move only according to its Hess movement pattern.



A movement pattern may include:

directional movement,

limited movement,

jumping,

special movement,

conditional movement,

state-dependent movement,

or movement that changes according to the piece's lifecycle.



---

11. CAPTURE

A capture occurs when a player's piece legally moves onto a square occupied by an opposing piece and the rules permit that capture.

The captured piece is removed from active play unless a special Hess mechanic modifies the outcome.

Special interactions may cause:

another piece to be sacrificed,

an attacker to be displaced,

a piece to transform,

the turn to be retained,

or another state transition.


Therefore a capture must be processed through the game engine's state-transition system rather than being implemented as merely:

remove(target)


---

12. FRIENDLY PIECES

A player cannot normally land a moving piece on a square occupied by their own piece.

A friendly piece may instead:

block movement,

prevent landing,

become a special-action target,

or participate in a mechanic such as King Swap or Jester sacrifice.


Each special mechanic defines its own exceptions.


---

13. ATTACK

An attack is a situation in which a piece has a legal capture opportunity against an opposing piece under the current game state.

Attack detection must use Hess's movement rules.

A square being geometrically reachable is not automatically sufficient if another Hess rule prevents the action.


---

14. KING AND KING SAFETY

The King remains a strategically and mechanically significant piece.

A player's King must not ordinarily be left in a position where it is subject to an illegal capture.

However, Hess's rules introduce mechanics that differ from ordinary chess.

The exact relationship between:

check,

King movement,

King Swap,

special attacks,

and game-ending conditions


must therefore be evaluated according to Hess rules rather than copied wholesale from chess.


---

15. KING SWAP

King Swap is a fundamental Hess mechanic.

A player may exchange the position of their King with an eligible friendly piece when the conditions for a King Swap are satisfied.

Conceptually:

King: e1
Friendly piece: c2

        ↓ King Swap

King: c2
Piece: e1

The two pieces exchange positions.

The mechanic is not merely an emergency escape mechanism.

King Swap may serve multiple strategic purposes, including:

escaping threats,

repositioning the King,

activating a piece,

creating tactical opportunities,

changing access to important squares,

preparing future moves,

and manipulating the board's geometry.



---

16. KING SWAP LEGALITY

A King Swap must be validated as an actual game action.

The engine must determine whether the resulting position is legal.

A swap must not be treated as automatically legal simply because the destination contains a friendly piece.

The rebuilt engine must explicitly test:

the starting position,

the destination,

attacks affecting the resulting King position,

relevant blockers,

current game state,

remaining swap availability,

and any other applicable Hess restriction.



---

17. KING SWAP RESOURCE

King Swaps are limited according to Hess's current rules.

The player's remaining swap count must be part of the game state.

The exact starting allocation and all consumption rules belong to the Special Mechanics specification.

The implementation must not infer availability from the board alone.


---

18. PAWNS

Pawns have directional movement.

A pawn may have:

normal forward movement,

initial double movement,

diagonal capture,

and special interaction with King Swap.


Pawn movement must depend on the pawn's relevant history/state where the rules require it.

Important V3 requirement

The engine must distinguish between:

Pawn currently on starting rank

and:

Pawn has never moved

if a future rule requires that distinction.

A King Swap must not accidentally erase or invent movement history.


---

19. PAWN DOUBLE-MOVE — PROVISIONAL IMPLEMENTATION RULE

The current implementation has exhibited cases where a pawn can incorrectly perform a two-square movement after it should no longer qualify.

For V3:

> A pawn's initial double movement must be tied to its actual starting-state eligibility, not merely to its current board coordinate.



A pawn that has lost its original eligibility must not regain it simply because it occupies a square from which a double movement would geometrically fit.

King Swap interaction

The exact question of whether a never-moved pawn that changes squares through King Swap retains its initial double-step eligibility remains a Hess design question.

Until explicitly resolved:

> Provisional rule: movement history belongs to the pawn itself, not the square it occupies.



Therefore, moving a pawn through a King Swap does not by itself mark the pawn as having made a normal pawn move.

This provisional rule should be tested extensively before being declared permanent.


---

20. MIDFIELD AND PAWNS

The midfield exists as a special spatial boundary in Hess.

It must not automatically be interpreted as a general pawn-movement barrier.

Unless another rule explicitly states otherwise:

> The midfield does not invalidate a pawn's ordinary directional movement merely because the pawn crosses it.



Any special interaction involving the midfield must be explicitly defined.


---

21. JESTER

The Jester is a lifecycle-based Hess piece rather than simply another static piece.

Its behavior may change according to:

its direction,

its position,

whether it has reached its home rank,

and its interaction with enemy pieces.


The Jester therefore requires persistent state beyond its board coordinate.

Detailed Jester movement belongs to Document 3.


---

22. JESTER LIFECYCLE

The current conceptual lifecycle is:

Pawn
 ↓
Promotion
 ↓
Jester
 ↓
Journey toward home
 ↓
Home-rank decision
 ↓
Remain Jester / Transform
 ↓
Potential return journey
 ↓
Potential Returning Pawn state

This lifecycle is one of Hess's defining mechanics.

The implementation must not reduce the Jester to an ordinary promoted piece.


---

23. JESTER SACRIFICE

When an enemy attack triggers the Jester's special deflection/sacrifice interaction, the defending player may be required to sacrifice an eligible friendly piece.

The sacrificed piece is removed.

The attacking piece may then be deflected/bounced back according to Hess's rules.

This interaction may also affect turn ownership.


---

24. JESTER SACRIFICE STATE

Jester sacrifice is treated as an explicit intermediate game state.

Conceptually:

Enemy attacks Jester
        ↓
Jester interaction triggered
        ↓
JESTER_SACRIFICE
        ↓
Player chooses sacrifice
        ↓
Sacrifice resolved
        ↓
Attacker deflected
        ↓
Turn determined

The engine must not attempt to compress this into an ordinary capture.


---

25. NO-ELIGIBLE-SACRIFICE CONDITION

A critical edge case occurs when the Jester interaction requires a sacrifice but the player has no eligible piece available.

The game must never enter an unrecoverable selection state.

Provisional V3 rule

If no valid sacrifice exists:

1. The sacrifice selection state is skipped.


2. The engine resolves the Jester interaction according to the no-sacrifice outcome.


3. The game continues normally according to the resulting state.



The exact final resolution remains subject to further rule confirmation.

The important invariant is:

> No legal position may permanently freeze because the UI expects an impossible sacrifice choice.




---

26. QUEEN

The Queen has special spatial restrictions that distinguish her from a conventional chess Queen.

The Queen is associated with the player's own half of the board and the midfield.

The Queen's detailed movement and raid rules belong to Document 3 and the Special Mechanics specification.


---

27. QUEEN MIDFIELD RESTRICTION

Under normal conditions, the Queen cannot freely cross the midfield.

The midfield therefore functions as a goalkeeper boundary for the Queen.

The restriction applies specifically to the Queen.

It must not be generalized to unrelated pieces.


---

28. QUEEN DESPERATION RAID

The Queen possesses a special raid mechanic that becomes available under a specific low-material condition.

The current documented concept is:

Only King + Queen remain
        ↓
Queen Raid becomes available
        ↓
Queen enters enemy territory
        ↓
Limited raid moves
        ↓
Queen returns to launch position

The Queen's raid therefore represents a temporary exception to her normal midfield restriction.

The exact trigger conditions, move counting, launch-state preservation, and interruption behavior must be treated as explicit game state rather than inferred from board coordinates.


---

29. SPECIAL PIECES

Hess contains pieces whose movement differs substantially from conventional chess.

These currently include:

King

Queen

Missile Rook

Staircase Bishop

T-Shape Knight

Pawn

Jester


The complete movement specifications are intentionally delegated to Document 3.


---

30. SPECIAL MOVEMENT PRINCIPLE

A piece's movement must be calculated from board coordinates, not fragile linear-array assumptions.

For example, a movement described as:

> “four squares horizontally”



must mean exactly four columns on the same rank.

It must never accidentally mean:

> “add four to an array index.”



This principle is particularly important for pieces such as the Missile Rook.


---

31. PROMOTION

A pawn reaching its relevant promotion condition may transform according to Hess's promotion rules.

Promotion may produce a Jester rather than following conventional chess promotion behavior.

The exact transformation rules belong to Document 3 and the Jester specification.


---

32. PIECE CAPS

Hess may restrict the number of certain pieces a player can possess.

These caps must be enforced by the engine rather than merely by the interface.

This becomes particularly important when a Jester reaches its home rank and offers a transformation choice.

The UI may display only legal choices, but the server/engine must independently validate them.


---

33. SPECIAL GAME STATES

Hess contains situations where a player is not simply choosing an ordinary movement.

The engine must support explicit states such as:

SETUP
PLAYING
JESTER_SACRIFICE
JESTER_HOME_CHOICE
QUEEN_RAID
GAME_OVER

Additional states may be introduced where required.

A state should exist whenever the rules require the player to make a decision before the ordinary turn can continue.


---

34. TURN RETENTION

Not every Hess action necessarily passes control to the opponent.

Some special mechanics can cause the current player to retain the turn.

This must be explicitly represented by the engine.

AI search must also simulate this correctly.

The AI must never assume:

every move → opponent turn

without examining the resulting game state.


---

35. DESPERATION STALE

Hess has a special game-ending condition referred to as Desperation Stale.

The concept is related to situations in which the King is under an unavoidable threat and normal defensive resources have been exhausted.

The engine must consider more than King escape squares.

Where applicable, it must also consider:

capturing the attacking piece,

blocking the attack,

and available Hess-specific defensive mechanisms.



---

36. BLOCKABLE ATTACKS

A blockable attack must be evaluated for available defensive interpositions.

This applies to relevant:

vertical attacks,

horizontal attacks,

diagonal attacks.


If a legal block exists, the position must not incorrectly be declared lost.

This is an explicit correction to the previous implementation.


---

37. STALEMATE

Hess must distinguish between:

A player having no legal ordinary movement

and

The player having no legal action whatsoever.

A player who has no legal movement but still has a legal Hess-specific action must not automatically be declared stalemated.

Examples may include:

an available King Swap,

an available pawn interaction,

or another rule-defined action.


Therefore stalemate detection must operate on all legal actions, not merely ordinary piece movement.


---

38. STALEMATE DRAW

Provisional canonical behavior

If:

the current player has no legal actions,

the King is not under a winning attack,

and no special action is available,


the game ends in a draw.

The exact distinction between ordinary stalemate and Desperation Stale remains part of the dedicated game-end specification.


---

39. TWO-KING ENDGAME

When only the two Kings remain:

White King
+
Black King

Hess enters a special countdown state.

The current documented value is:

> 30 half-moves



Each completed King move reduces the remaining count.

If the counter reaches zero without the required decisive event:

> The game is drawn.




---

40. COUNTDOWN STATE

The countdown must be part of the authoritative game state.

It must not exist solely as a visual UI timer.

The client displays the value received from the authoritative state.


---

41. REPETITION

Repeated positions can occur naturally in Hess because of:

King movement,

unusual piece mobility,

King Swap,

special movement patterns,

defensive play,

and the absence of a conventional chess-style timer.


Repetition handling therefore requires explicit consideration.

The AI should discourage meaningless repetition where it has a better alternative.

The exact official game-wide repetition rule remains to be finalized.

Until then:

> AI repetition avoidance is an evaluation concern, not automatically a game-ending rule.




---

42. NO TIMER

Hess currently does not require a chess-style move clock.

A player is not defeated merely because they take a long time to decide.

The game therefore relies on:

rule-based game endings,

special countdown mechanics where explicitly defined,

and future platform-level policies if time controls are eventually introduced.


A future competitive mode may introduce a timer without changing the core Hess rules.


---

43. GAME END

The game may end through a Hess-defined decisive condition or draw condition.

The engine must explicitly determine:

ONGOING
DRAW
PLAYER 1 WIN
PLAYER 2 WIN
find another legal move.
44. NO-LEGAL-MOVE SAFETY
A fundamental engine invariant is:
Every reachable game state must have a defined outcome or a valid next action.
If a player has no legal actions, the engine must resolve the position instead of leaving the game waiting indefinitely.
This prevents the class of freezes observed in earlier implementations.
45. ILLEGAL ACTION HANDLING
If a client requests an illegal action:
the server rejects it,
the authoritative game state remains unchanged,
the client receives an appropriate error/result,
and the game remains playable.
Illegal client input must never corrupt the game state.
46. HIDDEN INFORMATION SECURITY
Private information must remain private at the networking layer.
In particular:
hidden setups,
private player credentials,
internal AI information,
and other secret state
must not be transmitted to an opposing player unless the rules require disclosure.
Visual hiding is not sufficient.
47. RULES VS UI
The UI must communicate the rules but must not define them.
For example:
UI says:
"These squares are legal."

Engine says:
"These squares are legal."
If they disagree, the engine wins.
The long-term objective is to make disagreement impossible by deriving UI movement information from the same authoritative rule system.
48. RULES VS AI
The AI must use the same canonical rules engine.
It must not maintain an independent interpretation of Hess.
Conceptually:
Human Move
     ↓
Hess Engine

AI Move
     ↓
Hess Engine
The AI may evaluate positions differently from a human, but it must never operate under a different ruleset.
49. PROVISIONAL RULE HANDLING
When an unresolved rule prevents implementation from progressing, the development team should:
Identify the ambiguity.
Record it.
Define a provisional behavior.
Implement it consistently.
Add tests.
Playtest it.
Revisit the decision.
A provisional rule should therefore be easy to replace.
50. RULE DISCOVERY
New discoveries during development or gameplay should not automatically modify the engine.
Instead:
Discovery
   ↓
Question
   ↓
Does current rule already answer it?
   │
   ├── YES → Implement/test accordingly
   │
   └── NO
         ↓
     Design discussion
         ↓
     Provisional or Canonical decision
         ↓
     Update specification
         ↓
     Update engine/tests
This prevents bugs from becoming accidental rules.
51. HESS TERMINOLOGY — V3 WORKING VOCABULARY
The following terminology is introduced as a working vocabulary for the project.
These names may be refined later.
Move
A standard legal movement action performed by a piece.
Capture
A legal action that removes an opposing piece.
Swap
A special action exchanging the positions of two eligible friendly pieces.
King Swap
A Swap involving the player's King and another eligible friendly piece.
Sacrifice
Voluntarily or mandatorily removing one's own piece as part of a Hess mechanic.
Deflection / Bounce-Back
A special interaction in which an attacking piece is forced away from its current position following a Jester interaction.
Home Rank
The rank associated with the Jester's return destination.
Midfield
The boundary between ranks 4 and 5.
Raid
A temporary Queen state allowing her to operate beyond her normal territory.
Launchpad
The Queen's stored pre-raid position to which she returns after completing the raid.
Special State
A game state requiring rules beyond ordinary movement.
Legal Action
Any action currently permitted by Hess, including special actions.
These terms are project vocabulary, not necessarily the final player-facing wording.
52. RULES THAT REQUIRE FURTHER VALIDATION
The following areas are intentionally not treated as permanently frozen:
Pawn + King Swap
Whether a never-moved pawn retains double-step eligibility after being swapped with the King.
Current provisional rule: movement history belongs to the pawn.
Jester No-Sacrifice Resolution
The precise outcome when a Jester is attacked but the owner has no eligible sacrifice.
Current invariant: the game must not freeze.
Jester Home Behavior
The complete movement and state behavior after reaching home rank, including the exact transition between Jester states and Returning Pawn behavior.
Queen Raid
Exact trigger, interruption, counting, return behavior, and interactions with other mechanics.
Desperation Stale
The complete relationship between:
check,
blocking,
capturing,
King Swap,
and other defensive actions.
Repetition
Whether repeated positions eventually cause an official draw, and if so, under what exact conditions.
53. ENGINE INVARIANTS
Regardless of future rule refinements, the V3 engine must maintain these invariants:
Invariant 1
No illegal move may alter authoritative state.
Invariant 2
No legal game state may permanently freeze.
Invariant 3
The server and AI must use the same rules engine.
Invariant 4
Special mechanics must have explicit state transitions.
Invariant 5
Piece history required by a rule must be preserved.
Invariant 6
Hidden information must remain hidden at the networking layer.
Invariant 7
The UI must never be the ultimate authority on legality.
Invariant 8
Future rule changes must be possible without replacing the entire game engine.
54. IMPLEMENTATION INTERPRETATION
The implementation team should read this document as:
The current behavioral contract of Hess.
If existing code disagrees with this document, the disagreement must be investigated.
The solution is not automatically to change the document to match the code.
Instead determine:
Is the code wrong?
Is the document wrong?
Is the rule ambiguous?
Is a new rule needed?
Only then should the implementation change.
55. FINAL RULE PRINCIPLE
Hess is intentionally more open-ended than an ancient, decades-refined ruleset.
That is not a weakness of the project.
It is part of its current stage.
The objective of V3 is therefore not to pretend that every possible interaction has already been discovered.
The objective is to create a rules system that is:
precise where known,
deterministic where necessary,
honest about uncertainty,
testable under unusual conditions,
and
flexible enough to evolve.
Hess should be stable enough to play, but alive enough to discover.
